    84	**1** **INTRODUCTION**
    85	
    86	
    87	Widely-used programming languages have been conceived to be
    88	used by following either the imperative or object-oriented paradigm,
    89	_e.g.,_ C or Java. Some of these programming languages have also
    90	adopted constructs inspired by functional programming. This is the
    91	case of Java with the introduction of lambda functions in Java 1.8,
    92	Javascript with the introduction of higher-order functions, and,
    93	last but not least, Python. Such languages are not intrinsically
    94	functional (like Haskell or Scala), but give developers the flexibility
    95	to use functional constructs.
    96	
    97	Functional constructs have been advocated to help parallelization, avoiding the introduction of side effects in the source code [ 12,
    98	41 ]. At the same time, the understandability of such constructs
    99	has been questioned and discussed in previous studies and gray
   100	literature [13, 23, 26, 39].
   101	Let us focus on Python, which is, to date, Github’s second most
   102	used language [ 10 ]. Previous research has found that changes to
   103	some Pythonic functional constructs (lambdas, comprehensions,
   104	and map/reduce/filters) induce more fixes than other changes [ 44 ].
   105	Despite the discussions in gray literature and the results by Zampetti _et al._ [ 44 ], there is a lack of empirical evidence about the
   106	extent to which developers encounter more or less trouble when
   107	using functional constructs than when using their procedural alternative. To bridge this gap, this paper reports the results of a
   108	controlled experiment conducted with 209 paid developers, investigating the extent to which developers encounter difficulties while
   109	understanding and modifying code snippets involving functional
   110	constructs, as opposed to their procedural alternatives. The study
   111	focuses on lambdas, comprehensions, and map/reduce/filter (MRF)
   112	functions, as in previous work [ 44 ]. While, as pointed out by previous work, there are other Pythonic functional constructs one could
   113	consider, we preferred to focus on these because of their wide usage
   114	in Python [ 1, 6 ], and because of the inherent task complexity limitations when conducting a controlled experiment. The 209 developers
   115	have been recruited through the Prolific [ 15 ] platform. Specifically,
   116	each participant in the experiment had to:
   117	(1) Perform change tasks on functional, as well as procedural
   118	code (for which somebody else received the functional alternative);
   119	(2) Provide a perceived, comparative level of understandability
   120	over code snippets written in both fashions; and
   121	(3) Provide qualitative insights about the rationale for using (or
   122	not) the studied functional constructs in everyday development tasks.
   123	
   124	Findings of the study highlight that: (i) when modifying existing
   125	code snippets, developers perform better with lambdas than with
   126	
   127	
   128	ICSE ’24, April 14–20, 2024, Lisbon, Portugal Cyrine Zid, Fiorella Zampetti, Giuliano Antoniol, and Massimiliano Di Penta
   129	
   130	
   131	
   132	their procedural alternatives; instead, comprehensions make change
   133	tasks worse than their procedural alternatives; (ii) procedural code
   134	is generally perceived as more readable than its functional alternative; and (iii) developers found functional constructs useful to
   135	shorten the source code, even if they are challenging to debug.
   136	Results of the study provide empirical evidence on the difficulties
   137	experienced by developers when using functional constructs, also
   138	depending on their type and complexity. This has implications for
   139	developers’ education, prioritizing quality assurance on portions of
   140	code involving certain constructs, and aiding developers to properly
   141	use certain, complex-to-use constructs.
